📘 Method & Constructor References (Interview Notes)

Syntax idea: use `::` instead of a lambda that only calls an existing method/constructor.

────────────────────────────────────────
✅ Types

| Type | Syntax | Equivalent Lambda |
| ---- | ------ | ----------------- |
| Static method | `ClassName::staticMethod` | `(args) -> ClassName.staticMethod(args)` |
| Instance method of object | `object::instanceMethod` | `(args) -> object.instanceMethod(args)` |
| Instance method of class (unbound) | `ClassName::instanceMethod` | `(obj, args) -> obj.instanceMethod(args)` |
| Constructor | `ClassName::new` | `() -> new ClassName()` / `(a) -> new ClassName(a)` |

────────────────────────────────────────
📘 Quick examples

1) Static
`Consumer<String> printer = Util::print;`
same as `msg -> Util.print(msg)`

2) Instance of object
`list.forEach(System.out::println);`
same as `s -> System.out.println(s)`

3) Unbound instance
`Function<String, Integer> len = String::length;`
same as `str -> str.length()`

4) Constructor
`Supplier<List<String>> s = ArrayList::new;`
same as `() -> new ArrayList<>()`

`PersonFactory f = Person::new;`
same as `name -> new Person(name)`
(Java chooses constructor by functional method signature)

────────────────────────────────────────
✅ When to use
- Passing method/constructor into stream / factory / higher-order API
- Lambda has no extra logic (only calls method/ctor)

✅ Benefits
- Shorter than lambda
- More readable
- Same behavior as the matching lambda

────────────────────────────────────────
🎤 Interview traps
1) `Person::new` can mean no-arg OR 1-arg — depends on target functional interface.
2) Constructor/method reference cannot contain extra logic (then use lambda).
3) Unbound `String::length` still needs an instance at call time (`apply("Java")`).
4) Target must be a functional interface (one abstract method).
